SUMMARY:Algebraically free Markov chains (Paolo Perrone)
LOCATION:LG23\, Computer Science
DESCRIPTION:Paolo Perrone\nTheoretical Computer Science Seminar\n\nAlgebrai
 cally free monoids\, and more generally monads\, are commonly used to mode
 l operations that can be 'folded'\, such as repeated actions of a set of '
 elementary moves'. Notable examples are the writer monad (free over the al
 phabet set)\, and the free monad over a graph. In category theory\, an alg
 ebraically free monoid over an object X is a monoid FX together with a bij
 ection between X-algebras and FX-algebras\, with the intuition that FX-alg
 ebras are "just X-algebras where the action is iterated". In the theory of
  dynamical systems and Markov chains\, algebras can be decategorified to i
 nvariant states or invariant measures. Similarly\, we can decategorify a m
 onoid object to an idempotent…\n\nDetails: https://researchseminars.org/
 talk/TheoryCSBham/91/
